data.content:
This is a **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

## What happened

In C# 14, new built-in `Span<T>` conversions cause `array.Contains(num)` to resolve to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains`** (a span-based method) instead of **`Enumerable.Contains`**. `MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in a LINQ expression tree**, so it throws at runtime when the expression is compiled/interpreted.

## How to fix

Force the compiler to bind to the `Enumerable.Contains` overload by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevent C# 14 from choosing the span overload and keep the call within the expression-tree-compatible `System.Linq.Enumerable` API.
